import{_ as t,o,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"UXD-050 — Booster landing inset on /fly","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-050-booster-landing-inset-on-fly.md","filePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-050-booster-landing-inset-on-fly.md"}'),a={name:"uxs/decisions/UXD-050-booster-landing-inset-on-fly.md"};function i(r,e,d,l,c,h){return o(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="uxd-050-—-booster-landing-inset-on-fly" tabindex="-1">UXD-050 — Booster landing inset on /fly <a class="header-anchor" href="#uxd-050-—-booster-landing-inset-on-fly" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;UXD-050 — Booster landing inset on /fly&quot;">​</a></h1><ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: accepted (built; unit and e2e tests, see Verification; not checked on a real GPU, see Not done); partly superseded by <a href="./UXD-052-booster-returns-play-full-frame-when-the-entry-burn-is-sourced">UXD-052</a>: a drone-ship or Falcon 9 landing-zone landing with a sourced or approximate entry burn plays full frame, no longer as an inset. The inset stays for tower catches, Falcon Heavy side boosters and landings without an entry-burn time</li><li><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-11-22 (proposed) · 2026-11-22 (decided)</li><li><strong>Decider</strong>: the operator, 2026-11-22 — chose &quot;inset at real time&quot; from the three options below (plan row F2 of <code>docs/wip/2026-11-21-0.9.11-plan.md</code>)</li><li><strong>Answers</strong>: 0.9.11 plan row F2 (&quot;Booster landings, more&quot;)</li><li><strong>Builds on</strong>: <a href="./UXD-046-the-launch-runs-a-seeded-camera-director">UXD-046</a> (the launch scene and its camera director), <a href="./UXD-042-plan-to-fly-handoff-grouped-destinations-and-the-journey-bar">UXD-042</a> (the flight-plan beats and the play-bar marker row)</li><li><strong>Changes</strong>: <a href="./../UXS-003">UXS-003</a> (Booster landing inset), <code>05_Design_System.md</code> §5.12 (the launch scene), TA.md (Launch / powered ascent), <a href="./../IA">IA</a> §surfaces (<code>/fly</code>), <code>src/lib/three/README.md</code>, <code>src/lib/three/landing-inset.ts</code>, <code>engine-plume.ts</code>, <code>ascent-renderer.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/components/LaunchScene.svelte</code>, <code>src/lib/orbital/flight-plan-beats.ts</code>, <code>ascent-hud.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/physics/ascent/ascent-physics.ts</code></li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The launch scene turned a recovered booster tail-down at stage separation and let it drop away (0.9.10). A real booster lands minutes later: Falcon 9 about 8 to 9.5 minutes after liftoff, a Falcon Heavy side booster at T+8:17, Super Heavy about 7 minutes after liftoff. The scene&#39;s separation drop lasts about 5 s, so the landing had no time of its own. The question was where the landing is drawn and when.</p><p>The data is per flight, not per vehicle: Hera&#39;s booster and all three Europa Clipper cores were expended, Psyche&#39;s side boosters landed on LZ-1 and LZ-2, Inspiration4&#39;s booster landed on a drone ship. The sources are in <code>docs/wip/2026-11-22-booster-recovery-sourced.md</code>.</p><h2 id="options" tabindex="-1">Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Options&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Compress everything into the separation drop (about 5 s).</strong> The landing plays right after separation. For: no new element, no second view. Against: it shows the landing minutes before it happens, so the mission clock and the picture disagree. Declined by the operator.</li><li><strong>A camera cut to the booster at the real time.</strong> The main view leaves the ascent for the landing. For: the landing is full size. Against: it takes the main view, and the ascent HUD with it, away from the upper stage while the mission is still flying. Declined by the operator.</li><li><strong>A small inset at the real time.</strong> The main view stays with the upper stage; a framed inset shows the landing at the flight&#39;s real touchdown time. For: the clock and the picture agree, the ascent keeps its view. Against: the booster is small, and it needs its own scene and a free band of the screen. Chosen.</li></ol><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Option 3. At the flight&#39;s real booster-landing time a small inset shows the recovered booster coming down on its engines onto a drone ship, a landing zone, or into the launch tower&#39;s two catch arms (Super Heavy). The main view stays with the upper stage.</p><ol><li><strong>When.</strong> The inset opens 8 s before the landing burn (<code>INSET_LEAD_S</code>) and closes 4 s after touchdown (<code>LANDING_HOLD_S</code>). Where no source gives the burn start it is 25 s before touchdown (<code>LANDING_BURN_DEFAULT_S</code>). The launch act is held until the last inset closes (<code>holdUntilS</code> of <code>launchActEndS</code>); DART&#39;s booster lands at T+8:52, after its injection beat would end.</li><li><strong>What it shows.</strong> The landing-burn plume, and the legs swinging out in the last 4 s. The site is a drone ship, a landing zone or the tower&#39;s catch arms. A booster the flight expended gets no inset and tumbles.</li><li><strong>Caption.</strong> The HUD label and the site names, for example <code>BOOSTER LANDING · LZ-1 · LZ-2</code>, <code>BOOSTER LANDING · Of Course I Still Love You</code>, <code>TOWER CATCH</code>.</li><li><strong>Where.</strong> Right-aligned, 22 px from the right edge, in the free band under the FLIGHT PLAN list and above the play bar (above the SPEED and ALTITUDE readouts on phones). 7:5, at most 280 px wide (46 % of the width on narrow screens), shrunk to fit; not drawn when the band is under 90 px. It stays when the HUD is hidden, because it is scene content. The sky colour follows the launch scene&#39;s light (night, dusk, golden, midday).</li><li><strong>Flight plan.</strong> A BOOSTER LANDING or TOWER CATCH beat sits at touchdown, in time order, in the HUD list and as a marker on the play bar. Two new UI strings, <code>hud_booster_landing</code> and <code>hud_tower_catch</code>, in 14 locales.</li><li><strong>Per flight.</strong> Recovery is data on the launch profile: a vehicle default (<code>recovery</code>) and a per-mission override (<code>recoveryByMission</code>). Each entry says the part, the outcome (landed, caught, expended), the site, the site names, the touchdown time and its class (sourced, approx, typical).</li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>One renderer draws both views: the inset&#39;s own <code>THREE.Scene</code> is drawn into the frame&#39;s rectangle after the composed frame (scissor and viewport). No second canvas, no second GL context. The inset scene is built about 20 s before it opens.</li><li>The per-vehicle <code>RECOVERED</code> table in <code>launcher-models.ts</code> is gone. Hera&#39;s booster and the three Europa Clipper cores tumble instead of turning tail-down.</li><li>Falcon 9 landing legs (also Falcon Heavy side boosters and New Glenn) carry <code>userData.landingLegAngle</code> so the inset can hinge them.</li><li>The plume geometry moved from <code>ascent-scene.ts</code> to <code>engine-plume.ts</code> and is shared.</li><li>Two fixes shipped in the same arc, both about the separation time. <code>LaunchScene</code> now knows the mission id, so the per-mission SECO-to-separation delay (Inspiration4 198 s, Polaris Dawn 197 s) reaches the scene&#39;s own separation, not only the play-bar markers. A timed spacecraft separation counted its published delay from the end of the ascent integration, which runs a 90 s engine-dark coast past SECO, so every L63 separation was drawn 90 s late; it counts from SECO now. Inspiration4: SECO T+8:18, separation T+11:36 (was T+13:06). OTV-1 (Atlas V): SECO T+11:46, separation T+14:35.</li><li>It rules out a full-size landing view in this act. A cut to the booster would be a new decision.</li></ul><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unit tests: <code>src/lib/three/landing-inset.test.ts</code> (pose, window, placement), <code>src/lib/physics/ascent/recovery.test.ts</code>, <code>flight-plan-beats.test.ts</code>, <code>ascent-hud.test.ts</code>, <code>ascent-scene.test.ts</code>. E2e: <code>tests/e2e/fly-launch.spec.ts</code> &quot;booster landing is per flight&quot;.</p><h2 id="not-done" tabindex="-1">Not done <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-done" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not done&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Frame time with the inset on a real GPU or phone is not measured.</li><li>Times marked approx or typical in the sourced table are not the flight&#39;s own published times (USSF-52, IM-1, Beresheet and Nusantara Satu landing times were not found).</li><li>Starship missions that have not flown (<code>starship-demo</code>, <code>starship-mars-crew</code>) carry an approximate catch time (about T+7:00); F3 starts from the latest real flight.</li></ul>',16)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
